      "math_academy_id": "q-106269",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Find the equation of the parabola whose vertex is at $(-2,1)$ and whose leading coefficient is $1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola, and $a$ is the leading coefficient.\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-2,1)$ and $a=1$ into the above and then expanding and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1(x-(-2))^{2}+1 \\\\  & =(x+2)^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4x+4+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is $y={x}^{2}+4x+5.$",
